{"type": "text", "content": "Rethinking the primitives"}
{"type": "text", "content": "AI products have accumulated a large vocabulary in a short time. Chats, sessions, models, context windows, memories, system prompts, projects, skills, connectors, agents, tools, sandboxes, permissions, and automations all describe real parts of these systems."}
{"type": "text", "content": "But exposing each one as a separate product concept asks users to understand more than they need to. We started by asking which concepts a person actually needs in order to work with an agent."}
{"type": "text", "content": "We kept coming back to five:"}
{"type": "text", "content": "Bots are persistent agents with their own identity, memory, runtime, and tools."}
{"type": "text", "content": "Chats are the conversational interface for working with a Bot."}
{"type": "text", "content": "Prompts give a Bot context or instructions. They can be used once, saved as Skills, or triggered automatically as Routines."}
{"type": "text", "content": "Tools let Bots access information and take action through software, APIs, connectors, the shell, or computer use."}
{"type": "text", "content": "Artifacts are the documents, designs, code, data, and other durable outputs that Bots create or modify."}
{"type": "text", "content": "Everything else could remain beneath the interface until the user had a reason to care about it. The next question was which of these five objects should organize the product."}
{"type": "text", "content": "From chat history to a Bot roster"}
{"type": "text", "content": "Chats are disposable. We start a conversation to solve a problem. It gets pushed down the sidebar. A week later, we start another one. You rarely go back beyond the most recent five."}
{"type": "text", "content": "That behavior is perfectly reasonable when the unit of interaction is a question. It becomes strange when the thing on the other side of the interaction is supposed to know you, remember previous work, and take responsibility over time."}
{"type": "text", "content": "So the main objects in Grok Bot are Bots, not conversations. A Bot has a name. It has an avatar and a title. It remembers its conversations with you. It has its own computer and tools. When you come back tomorrow, you are coming back to the same Bot."}
{"type": "text", "content": "Project Acme"}
