{"type": "text", "content": "Presence as interface"}
{"type": "text", "content": "Once a Bot was something you maintain over time rather than a session you start, the way Bots appear in the product had to answer three questions at once:"}
{"type": "text", "content": "Who is this?"}
{"type": "text", "content": "What are they doing?"}
{"type": "text", "content": "How much do I need to know?"}
{"type": "text", "content": "Who is this"}
{"type": "text", "content": "A roster only works if it can be scanned quickly. As the roster grows, we did not want people to have to read every name each time they opened the product. They should be able to recognize a Bot from its avatar almost peripherally."}
{"type": "figure", "img": "https://x.ai/_next/static/media/initials-1.0yig-qj1joruk.png?dpl=b542f0a51fdbb354684e4e23c9b14ce95dbf39f7", "caption": "Bot avatar visual style explorations by Kenny Kuh and Peng Zheng", "after_para": 84, "hero": false}
{"type": "text", "content": "At the same time, we wanted to keep the avatars consistent enough to read as one system. We studied character systems across illustration, animation, games, and interface design, exploring everything from initials and emojis to pixel art, watercolor, claymorphism, Noritake-style line art, silhouettes, and identicons."}
{"type": "text", "content": "Most approaches solved one side of the problem better than the other. Watercolor and clay gave individual Bots plenty of character but carried too much detail at sidebar scale. Simpler systems sat more naturally in the interface, but often left the Bots looking interchangeable."}
{"type": "text", "content": "The system we landed on keeps the basic construction consistent, using simple shapes and expressive eyes, then introduces distinction through controlled variations and accessories. Each Bot remains recognizable at a glance without appearing to come from a different visual world."}
{"type": "text", "content": "What are they doing"}
{"type": "text", "content": "Once the avatar became the Bot’s identity, it was also the natural place to show state. A Bot may be idle, thinking, working, waiting, blocked, or done. We could have represented each state with a separate indicator, but that would have added another layer of UI for the user to interpret."}
{"type": "text", "content": "Instead, we explored how much of the lifecycle the avatar itself could carry."}
{"type": "text", "content": "At rest, the Bot is calm and slightly curious. When work arrives, it acknowledges the task. As work begins, it kicks into gear. Its motion changes again when it is waiting or needs help, then settles once the work is done. The avatar now shows what the Bot is doing as well as which Bot it is."}
{"type": "text", "content": "How much do I need to know"}
{"type": "text", "content": "A related design question was how much of the Bot’s execution to show. One approach would have been the standard “three animated dots” but that would have been too little information, making it hard for users to tell whether the Bot was working or stuck."}
